## NOTES (audit_notes_not_execution_log — reasoning, not command output)
- checks.py produced three false positives on its first run against these same
  bytes and each rule was tightened before the logged run: (1) an 'internal
  double space' rule flagged the legitimate column padding after 'relé:' /
  'instalado:' and the two-space row indent inside resume_hint, and the shared
  design of 'search  /' — the rule now exempts runs after a colon, runs after a
  newline, and runs the English value also carries; (2) the English-residue
  word list contained 'no', which is also a Spanish word, so Spanish negations
  tripped it; (3) controls that needed a feature this namespace does not carry
  (a backtick span in tui.library) could not fire and now fall back to a minimal
  synthetic fixture. The logged run is the post-fix run and all eight controls
  fire.
- TWO INDEPENDENT PASSES were made on the Spanish text: pass 1 read es against
  en and the context sidecar key by key; pass 2 read the MESSAGE VALUES ALONE
  (no English in view) in file order, looking only for what stumbles in
  Spanish. The findings are the union of both; pass 2 added the wording notes
  on 'bajo profundidad' and 'Tiempo más grueso' that pass 1 had accepted.
- Meaning, register and terminology verdicts come from reading, not from the
  script; the script only proves the mechanical floor and that its own checks
  can fail.

